{"title":"Amazon’s iRobot purchase sucks up formal competition concerns in EU (TechCrunch, 2023-11-27T23:29:10+00:00)","text":"Amazon’s iRobot purchase sucks up formal competition concerns in EU (TechCrunch, 2023-11-27T23:29:10+00:00)
Today’s announcement by the EU, following several months of in-depth examination looking at how the transaction might impact competition, lays out formal concerns for Amazon to respond to. The EU’s objections focus on foreclosure risks.

The EU says its concerns focus on whether the deal will give Amazon the ability and incentive to foreclose iRobot’s rivals by deploying strategies aimed at preventing competitors from selling RVCs on Amazon’s marketplace and/or making it harder for them to do so — such as by delisting rival products; reducing their visibility in both non-paid (i.e. organic) and paid results (i.e. ads) displayed on its marketplace; limiting their access to certain widgets it operates (such as the “other products you may like” feature) or “certain commercially-attractive product labels” (e.g. ‘Amazon’s choice’ or ‘Works With Alexa’); and/or by directly or indirectly raising the costs of iRobot’s rivals to advertise and sell their RVCs on its marketplace.","entities":["RVCs","Amazon’s marketplace","Amazon’s choice","Works With Alexa","other products you may like","foreclosure risks","iRobot","EU","Amazon’s iRobot purchase sucks up formal competition concerns in EU","TechCrunch","Alexa","competition","Amazon"],"facts":["The 26 best Amazon Prime Day deals you can still shop for today -MENTIONS-> iRobot","iRobot -PARTICIPATED_IN-> Amazon’s iRobot purchase","iRobot -MENTIONS-> intense competition","A comprehensive list of 2023 tech layoffs -MENTIONS-> iRobot","iRobot -PARTICIPATED_IN-> lay off 7% of its workforce, roughly 85 employees"]}

{"title":"Elon Musk’s X faces first DSA probe in EU over illegal content risks, moderation, transparency and deceptive UX design (TechCrunch, 2023-12-18T13:04:56+00:00)","text":"Elon Musk’s X faces first DSA probe in EU over illegal content risks, moderation, transparency and deceptive UX design (TechCrunch, 2023-12-18T13:04:56+00:00)
The EU also said then it was looking at X’s compliance across a number of areas, including policies and practices regarding notices on illegal content, complaint handling, risk assessment and measures to mitigate the risks identified — and it gave X until the end of October to respond.

“On the basis of the preliminary investigation conducted so far, including on the basis of an analysis of the risk assessment report submitted by X in September, X’s Transparency report published on 3 November, and X’s replies to a formal request for information, which, among others, concerned the dissemination of illegal content in the context of Hamas’ terrorist attacks against Israel, the Commission has decided to open formal infringement proceedings against X under the Digital Services Act,” the EU said today.","entities":["risk assessment","illegal content","moderation","EU","TechCrunch","formal infringement proceedings","Hamas’ terrorist attacks against Israel","deceptive UX design","Elon Musk’s X faces first DSA probe in EU over illegal content risks, moderation, transparency and deceptive UX design","X","Elon Musk","Hamas","transparency","Commission","Israel"],"facts":["After warning, the EU opens investigation into X over alleged disinformation -MENTIONS-> risk assessment","After warning, the EU opens investigation into X over alleged disinformation -MENTIONS-> illegal content","EU fires urgent warning at Elon Musk’s X over illegal content and disinformation following Hamas attacks -MENTIONS-> illegal content","Why Mozilla is betting on a decentralized social networking future -MENTIONS-> illegal content","Meta says it’s prioritizing livestreaming checks during Israel-Hamas war -MENTIONS-> illegal content"]}

{"title":"In the end, the FTX trial was about the friends screwed along the way (The Verge, 2023-10-26T12:00:00+00:00)","text":"In the end, the FTX trial was about the friends screwed along the way (The Verge, 2023-10-26T12:00:00+00:00)
In it, Bankman-Fried tried to justify the missing funds through the margin loan program. It was such a flimsy excuse that even George Stephanopoulos could see through it.

Did Bankman-Fried really think he could get away with it all? He must still think so, or he would not be dragging his closest companions’ names through the mud in a jury trial. Throughout the entire prosecution’s case, the defense has struggled to shake the credibility of witnesses such as Yedidia, Wang, and Ellison. The hits the defense made on Singh didn’t make me doubt the overall truth of his story — they just made me think that Singh was kind of self-serving and trying to secure himself a lenient sentence.","entities":["Bankman-Fried","In the end, the FTX trial was about the friends screwed along the way","Ellison","Singh","missing funds","FTX trial","margin loan program","George Stephanopoulos","the defense","FTX","Wang","Yedidia","the prosecution","The Verge"],"facts":["Sam Altman backs teens’ startup, Google unveils the Pixel 8 and TikTok tests an ad-free tier -MENTIONS-> Bankman-Fried","SBF’s trial starts soon, but how did he — and FTX — get here? -MENTIONS-> Bankman-Fried","Sex, Signal messages, and sabotaging FTX's code: SBF execs and Bahamas roommates tell all in court -MENTIONS-> Bankman-Fried","Today the FTX jury suffered through a code review -MENTIONS-> Bankman-Fried","Bankman-Fried -SAID-> Alameda Research"]}

{"title":"Sam Bankman-Fried was a terrible boyfriend (The Verge, 2023-10-10T23:50:21+00:00)","text":"Sam Bankman-Fried was a terrible boyfriend (The Verge, 2023-10-10T23:50:21+00:00)
It was that Ellison’s calculations assumed Alameda could borrow $1.8 billion in normal dollars and $1.5 billion in crypto from FTX. The spreadsheet makes this clear with a row labeled “FTX borrows,” which Ellison said were customer funds.

Meanwhile, echoing Bankman-Fried and Ellison’s romantic relationship, FTX was keeping cozy private ties with Alameda yet publicly holding it at arm’s length. On January 14th, 2022, Bankman-Fried tweeted, “We’re launching a $2b venture fund: FTX Ventures!” Those funds came from Alameda, Ellison testified. But Bankman-Fried didn’t want to go public with the source of the funds. He said he thought Alameda’s brand was less good, and he didn’t want his name associated with it. Alameda also bought Robinhood shares for Bankman-Fried, who moved them to a vehicle called “Emergent Fidelity Technologies” to avoid association with Alameda.","entities":["crypto","Alameda","Sam Bankman-Fried was a terrible boyfriend","FTX Ventures","Emergent Fidelity Technologies","Ellison","customer funds","FTX","Sam Bankman-Fried","Robinhood","The Verge"],"facts":["SBF Trial: The latest updates from the FTX collapse’s courtroom drama -MENTIONS-> crypto","SBF, riding high on FTX, reportedly offered $5B to Trump to cancel President re-run -MENTIONS-> crypto","Is Sam Bankman-Fried a bad ‘man’ or a good ‘boy’? Lawyers swap opening statements before first witnesses take the stand -MENTIONS-> crypto","SBF’s trial starts soon, but how did he — and FTX — get here? -MENTIONS-> crypto","Is Sam Bankman-Fried’s defense even trying to win? -MENTIONS-> crypto"]}

{"title":"How ego and fear fuelled the rise of artificial intelligence (The Age, 2023-12-09T18:30:00+00:00)","text":"How ego and fear fuelled the rise of artificial intelligence (The Age, 2023-12-09T18:30:00+00:00)
“The co-founders themselves came to the conclusion that they wished to depart OpenAI to start their own company, made this known to OpenAI’s leadership, and over several weeks negotiated an exit on mutually agreeable terms.” Anthropic accepted a $US4 billion investment from Amazon and another $US2 billion from Google two years later. The Reveal After OpenAI received another $US2 billion from Microsoft, Altman and another senior executive, Greg Brockman, visited Bill Gates at his sprawling mansion on the shores of Lake Washington, outside Seattle. The Microsoft founder was no longer involved in the company day to day but kept in regular touch with its executives.

Over dinner, Gates told them he doubted that large language models could work. He would stay sceptical, he said, until the technology performed a task that required critical thinking – passing an Advanced Placement biology test, for instance. Five months later, on August 24, 2022, Altman and Brockman returned and brought along an OpenAI researcher named Chelsea Voss.","entities":["How ego and fear fuelled the rise of artificial intelligence","Altman","Bill Gates","Lake Washington","visit to Bill Gates","Chelsea Voss","Advanced Placement biology test","Microsoft","large language models","The Age","Google","Anthropic","OpenAI","Greg Brockman","Seattle"],"facts":["How ego and fear fuelled the rise of artificial intelligence -MENTIONS-> disinformation","How ego and fear fuelled the rise of artificial intelligence -MENTIONS-> Musk","How ego and fear fuelled the rise of artificial intelligence -MENTIONS-> Page","How ego and fear fuelled the rise of artificial intelligence -MENTIONS-> Mark Zuckerberg","How ego and fear fuelled the rise of artificial intelligence -MENTIONS-> Peter Thiel"]}

{"title":"In the end, the FTX trial was about the friends screwed along the way (The Verge, 2023-10-26T12:00:00+00:00)","text":"In the end, the FTX trial was about the friends screwed along the way (The Verge, 2023-10-26T12:00:00+00:00)
It is easy to lie with words and harder to lie with money. Bankman-Fried went around telling people that he wanted to make a lot of money to give it away, an idea of the Effective Altruism movement that Bankman-Fried affiliated himself with — but the amount of money he funneled to his Paper Bird vehicle alone was ten times the size of his philanthropic spending as outlined by Easton. And it was all vastly outweighed by his investments — the places where he could prove he was very smart by investing in the right companies early.

And in brief, explosive testimony, former FTX lawyer Can Sun demonstrated what Bankman-Fried’s tactic was. During the days in November when the exchange imploded, Bankman-Fried was trying to raise money from Apollo, the private equity group. Apollo wanted an explanation for what had happened, and so Bankman-Fried came to Sun to ask him to come up with justifications for the missing funds.","entities":["Easton","Bankman-Fried","In the end, the FTX trial was about the friends screwed along the way","Paper Bird","Effective Altruism movement","Can Sun","Apollo","missing funds","FTX trial","FTX","The Verge"],"facts":["SBF Trial: The latest updates from the FTX collapse’s courtroom drama -MENTIONS-> Easton","Easton -PARTICIPATED_IN-> SBF Trial","SBF’s trial starts soon, but how did he — and FTX — get here? -MENTIONS-> Bankman-Fried","Sam Altman backs teens’ startup, Google unveils the Pixel 8 and TikTok tests an ad-free tier -MENTIONS-> Bankman-Fried","Sex, Signal messages, and sabotaging FTX's code: SBF execs and Bahamas roommates tell all in court -MENTIONS-> Bankman-Fried"]}

