{"title":"Israel's defense undone by reliance on technology (Globes English | Israel Business Arena, 2023-11-05T13:35:33+00:00)","text":"Israel's defense undone by reliance on technology (Globes English | Israel Business Arena, 2023-11-05T13:35:33+00:00)
A comprehensive study conducted on intelligence failures in the national arena, from the fall of the Iron Curtain in 1989 to 2010, showed no improvement in providing or thwarting warnings about strategic surprises, even if the means of gathering information had improved. Four years after signing the study, former US President Barack Obama admitted that US intelligence had not correctly assessed the severity of the threat of large parts of Iraq and Syria being occupied by ISIS.

"No matter how technologically advanced your army or how effective your intelligence gathering system, identifying strategic surprises is a human process that depends on analyzing what is called, in the intelligence community, 'weak signals,'" Barnea says. "These are pieces of relevant information that have been conveyed to the organization, but because they are low-key, or because of the information overload around them, the organization fails to identify and interpret them correctly."","entities":["Globes English | Israel Business Arena","Barnea","Israel's defense undone by reliance on technology","intelligence gathering","ISIS","fall of the Iron Curtain","US intelligence","strategic surprises","intelligence failures","weak signals","Syria","Iraq","Barack Obama","technology"],"facts":["Psagot worth 80% less since Rani Zim bought it -PUBLISHED_BY-> Globes English | Israel Business Arena",""There are no sacred numbers" -PUBLISHED_BY-> Globes English | Israel Business Arena","There are no sacred numbers -PUBLISHED_BY-> Globes English | Israel Business Arena","Office rents in Israel stay firm - for now -PUBLISHED_BY-> Globes English | Israel Business Arena","Israel's prince of public diplomacy -PUBLISHED_BY-> Globes English | Israel Business Arena"]}

{"title":"The people who ruined the internet (The Verge, 2023-11-01T13:00:00+00:00)","text":"The people who ruined the internet (The Verge, 2023-11-01T13:00:00+00:00)
In the beginning, the internet was seen as an improvement on the spirit of the public library. Here was an opportunity to transcend the gatekeepers controlling who could publish a book, allowing mankind to fully connect and share knowledge. Instead, we have ended up in a situation arguably worse than before, where nearly all online information runs through a single company, which assumes a veneer of civic utility, of impassive authority, when it is very much not a neutral entity.

“There were so many true believers at Google in the early days,” Cutts told me. “As companies get big, it gets harder to get things done. Inevitably, people start to think about profit or quarterly numbers.” He claimed that, at least while he was there, search quality always came before financial goals, but he believes that the public underestimates how Google is shaping what they see, saying, “I deeply, deeply, deeply believe search engines are newspaper-like entities, making editorial decisions.”","entities":["internet","The people who ruined the internet","Cutts","search engines","public library","Google","The Verge"],"facts":["How OpenAI's ChatGPT has changed the world in just a year -MENTIONS-> internet","ChatGPT can now browse the internet (again) -MENTIONS-> internet","Gen Z just saw the worst war in Israel in 50 years break out all over the internet, but they were raised on 'disturbing images' -MENTIONS-> internet","6 VCs explain how startups can capture and defend marketshare in the AI era -MENTIONS-> internet","California’s privacy watchdog eyes AI rules with opt-out and access rights -MENTIONS-> internet"]}

